course: dictionary
lesson: 7583
title: Words about stopping trouble
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Preventable is built from two parts you may know. Prevent means to stop something from happening, and -able means able to be. So preventable means able to be stopped before it happens.
explain: Use the context to check. «Tooth decay is almost always preventable.» Ask what would fit: brushing teeth and visiting the dentist could stop tooth decay, so preventable must mean it can be stopped. Swap your meaning into the sentence: "almost always able to be stopped before it happens" still makes sense.
explain: Preventable is often used about accidents and illness. Helmets, seatbelts and washing hands show that many injuries are preventable. The word tells us someone could have stopped the bad thing, so it was not simply bad luck.
fact: Preventable means able to be stopped before it happens.
fact: Prevent means to stop something from happening, and -able means able to be.
fact: «Tooth decay is almost always preventable.» Dentists call tooth decay caries.
fact: Phrases you may meet are «preventable disease» and «entirely preventable».
fact: Many injuries are preventable with helmets, seatbelts and washing hands.
exercise: «Tooth decay is almost always preventable.» What does preventable mean? | Impossible to see coming | Very painful | *Able to be stopped before it happens | Likely to get worse
exercise: Many injuries are preventable with helmets and seatbelts. What does this tell us? | Injuries cannot be stopped at all | Injuries only happen to careless people | Helmets and seatbelts are very expensive | *Helmets and seatbelts can help stop many injuries
exercise: Ella says preventable means very old. Is she right? | Yes, it means very old | *No, it means able to be stopped before it happens | Yes, it means glowing | No, it means barely visible
test: «The accident was entirely preventable.» What does this tell you? | It was a surprise that nobody expected | It happened to a very old car | *Someone could have stopped it from happening | It was very funny
test: Which phrase means a disease that could have been stopped? | Luminous disease | Famished disease | *Preventable disease | Ancient disease
exam: Which sentence uses preventable correctly? | The sky was preventable and very blue | Preventable is a colour we see in autumn | She ran preventable across the field | *Washing your hands is a simple way to stop many illnesses, so many illnesses are preventable
